1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,885
JAMIE: Microsoft just fired four thousand eight hundred people and is spinning off Xbox—a company saying 'we're not a gaming company' while still owning the thing gamers buy.

2
00:00:08,885 --> 00:00:42,524
ALEX: Tech is carrying the market today. Nasdaq up one point two nine percent to twenty-six thousand one hundred sixty-five, while the S&P is barely moving at point six nine percent to seventy-five thirty-four. The real story is who's winning: A M D jumped nearly ten percent, Tesla up four point nine two, Broadcom up three point six five. Meanwhile consumer discretionary is getting hammered—Procter & Gamble down two point seven nine, Home Depot down two point six nine. The V I X is cooling, sitting at sixteen point zero three, which tells you the market thinks the Fed is going to keep patience with rates.

3
00:00:42,524 --> 00:00:46,967
JAMIE: So A M D's having a day. What's driving that kind of move?

4
00:00:46,967 --> 00:01:13,624
ALEX: Chip demand tied to AI infrastructure buildout. Everyone's watching the capex wars—Microsoft, Meta, the whole cloud stack—and A M D makes the processors that feed it. When you've got two mega-cap companies essentially racing to stack servers with AI chips, the foundries and the chip makers ride that wave. Tesla's up on the same logic: if you're pricing in a Fed that won't aggressively tighten, growth stocks get cheaper money to borrow, and Tesla's the growth stock that also owns a battery-and-compute story.

5
00:01:13,624 --> 00:01:32,982
MAYA: But Procter & Gamble and Home Depot tanking—those are the companies that depend on actual consumers buying actual things. We're watching this split where the market is saying 'AI infrastructure? Yes, infinite capex forever.' But the moment someone needs to buy a box of detergent or fix their kitchen, suddenly we care about whether people have money in their pockets.

6
00:01:32,982 --> 00:01:49,484
ALEX: They're not selling it. They're spinning off four studios. They're keeping the I P and the revenue streams. What they're actually saying is 'we don't want to run this as a business unit anymore.' That's different from 'gaming is dead.' It's more like 'gaming is infrastructure now, not a profit center.'

7
00:01:49,484 --> 00:01:52,340
JAMIE: Infrastructure meaning what? They're keeping Xbox itself, right?

8
00:01:52,340 --> 00:02:08,524
MAYA: They're keeping Xbox, Game Pass, the platform. But the creative studios that make the games for those platforms—those are becoming separate entities. It's like owning a stadium but firing all the teams and saying 'we're a venue business now.' Except the venue is losing money because nobody's coming to watch.

9
00:02:08,524 --> 00:02:24,074
ALEX: Micron's stock is up on what Wall Street is calling a return to chip-sector optimism. The real read, though: investors are waiting for Samsung's earnings and S K Hynix's A D R listing to confirm whether this is genuine demand or just relief that the numbers weren't catastrophic.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/microns-stock-gains-signaling-a-return-to-optimism-about-the-chip-sector-0b7f6a8c?mod=mw_rss_topstories

10
00:02:24,074 --> 00:02:38,990
MAYA: So we're celebrating that a company didn't completely crater. That's the bar now—'Micron didn't implode, let's buy it.' The entire sector is basically a Schrödinger's cat where nobody opens the box until Samsung reports and then we find out if we were right or just lucky.

11
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:51,683
ALEX: A sitting president calling a sports federation to overturn a disciplinary decision is not a normal thing that happens. The fact that it worked is the story. F I F A could have said no. They chose not to.

12
00:02:51,683 --> 00:03:12,946
MAYA: The UK Financial Conduct Authority just warned of an arms race in AI deployment across finance—meaning everyone's shipping AI features faster than they're stress-testing them, and regulators are watching it happen in real time and basically saying 'we don't have the tools to stop this yet.' Millions of people are using AI for personal finance decisions and the agency running the watchdog is admitting they're behind.

13
00:03:12,946 --> 00:03:23,101
JAMIE: So the F C A is saying 'we see the problem, we understand the risk, we just can't actually do anything about it right now.' That's not a warning—that's a confession.

14
00:03:23,101 --> 00:03:37,699
ALEX: Canada's spy agency just published a report saying they hacked drug traffickers, ransomware gangs, and extremist groups last year. They're advertising their own hacking operations in an annual report. Either they're confident nobody can retaliate, or they're very worried about the optics of staying quiet.

15
00:03:37,699 --> 00:03:54,518
MAYA: Telling everyone you hacked them is such a specific move—it's like a bank robber calling the police to say 'I stole your evidence before you could use it against me.' If you're proud of it, fine, but the fact that you had to announce it suggests you were worried someone else would.

16
00:03:54,518 --> 00:04:12,607
MAYA: Nintendo is discontinuing the original Switch in Europe next year. Seven years after launch—the console that was supposed to be timeless is now officially aging out. They're replacing it with Switch 2, which has a replaceable battery, which is apparently what Europeans required in writing to get Nintendo to acknowledge that consumer hardware eventually stops working.

17
00:04:12,607 --> 00:04:24,031
JAMIE: So everyone who bought a Switch in 2017 is now a retro gamer without even trying. You didn't collect anything—you just kept it in your backpack for seven years and now you're a museum piece.

18
00:04:24,031 --> 00:04:42,437
ALEX: The replaceable battery is the real move. Nintendo's admitting the original design was disposable but calling it a feature upgrade. It's the same hardware philosophy they've shipped since the Game Boy—sell you something with a finite lifespan, then sell you the next one when the battery dies. Except now the EU made them admit it out loud.

19
00:04:42,437 --> 00:04:50,688
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: Regeneron reports earnings on July sixth. Biotech earnings season is thin, which means this one's going to move the entire sector.

20
00:04:50,688 --> 00:04:55,131
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
